    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "How to Recreate a Classic JRPG Using KGraph Parsing with Automated Playthrough",
      "dcterms:description": "Complete step-by-step interactive masterclass on using a browser and Python tools to harvest classic JRPG maps, wikis, PDF manuals, and 6502 ROM tables into a private local workspace, parsing them into an OSLC JSON-LD Knowledge Graph with robos-jrpg-parser, slicing 16x16 tile grids with indoor/outdoor palette decoding, and running headless Xvfb Cucumber (behave) video playthroughs in Godot 4.",
      "robos:difficulty": "Advanced",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "110 minutes",
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:docPath": "docs/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough.md",
      "robos:applicationTitle": "RobOS JRPG Parser & Godot 4 Playthrough Harness",
      "robos:technology": "Python 3 / robos-jrpg-parser / OSLC KGraph / Godot 4.3 / Cucumber Xvfb",
      "robos:ogImage": "https://rowbose.com/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/jrpg-acquisition-and-rom-pipeline.png",
      "robos:modulesCount": 6,
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:targetApplication": "urn:robos:app:kgraph-parse-portal",
      "robos:targetAudience": "Game Developers, Retro Preservationists, AI Agent Engineers, Context Engineers",
      "robos:topic": "Context-Engineered Classic JRPG Reconstruction & Automated Video Verification",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-11-04T18:44:28.276Z",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "id": "mod-01-browser-and-python-harvesting",
          "title": "Module 1: Browser Discovery, Reusable Manifest Extraction & Agent-Driven Python Harvesting",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Learn how to inspect any classic JRPG archive in a browser, extract asset URLs into a reusable sources-manifest.json (using Browser DevTools or Python HTML link discovery), run a game-agnostic Python harvester (harvest_jrpg_assets.py), and orchestrate the entire download-and-parse pipeline with RobOS Agents.",
          "lessonHtml": "\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🌐 1. The 5 Essential Browser Resource Categories for Any Classic JRPG</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            When reconstructing an 8-bit or 16-bit JRPG (whether <em>Final Fantasy</em>, <em>Dragon Warrior</em>, <em>Chrono Trigger</em>, <em>Phantasy Star</em>, or <em>EarthBound</em>), no single website holds all the data needed for a playable engine. Before writing a single line of game code, we inspect five complementary categories of public reference archives in a browser and organize them into a <strong>private local workspace</strong> (<code>~/Downloads/&lt;game-id&gt;/</code>) that is never committed to the public Git repository:\n          </p>\n          <table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:12px; margin:12px 0;\">\n            <thead>\n              <tr style=\"background:#21262d; color:#f0f6fc;\">\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Category</th>\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Where to Browse (Reusable Pattern)</th>\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Example (NES Final Fantasy)</th>\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Parser Role &amp; Local Folder</th>\n              </tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>1. Pixel-Exact Map Atlases</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>vgmaps.com/Atlas/&lt;System&gt;/index.htm#&lt;Game&gt;</code></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">1:1 pixel rips of Overworld, Coneria, Castle Coneria, Temple of Fiends, Marsh Cave, Earth Cave, Gurgu Volcano, Ice Cave, Sea Shrine, Floating Castle</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>world-map</code>, <code>area-map</code><br/>→ <code>~/Downloads/&lt;game&gt;/maps/</code></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>2. Wiki Reference &amp; Walkthrough HTML</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>strategywiki.org/wiki/&lt;Game&gt;/Table_of_Contents</code></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>Shops</code>, <code>Weapons</code>, <code>Armor</code>, <code>Spells</code>, <code>Enemies</code>, <code>Characters</code>, and walkthrough subpages + shop UI screenshots</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>walkthrough</code>, <code>shop-screen</code><br/>→ <code>~/Downloads/&lt;game&gt;/ui_shops/</code></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>3. Official Pack-In Manuals &amp; Charts</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Nintendo Classic Mini PDF archive / Archive.org manuals</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>CLV-P-NAAAE.pdf</code> (Official 84-page NES manual &amp; fold-out equipment/magic charts)</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>document</code><br/>→ <code>~/Downloads/&lt;game&gt;/manuals/</code></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>4. Chiptune &amp; OST Audio</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>zophar.net/music/&lt;system&gt;/&lt;game&gt;</code></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Original 2A03 <code>.nsf</code> archive + track-split MP3s (Prelude, Town, Cornelia Castle, Temple of Chaos)</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>music</code><br/>→ <code>~/Downloads/&lt;game&gt;/audio/</code></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>5. Mechanics &amp; Disassembly Tables</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">GameFAQs Mechanics Guides &amp; GitHub 6502/65816 Disassemblies</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">AstralEsper Game Mechanics Guide (v0.85) &amp; Disch 6502 Disassembly (monster bytes, 8-slot encounters, class growth, 48-byte palettes)</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><code>document</code><br/>→ <code>~/Downloads/&lt;game&gt;/rom_data/</code></td>\n              </tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🔍 2. Bridging Browser Discovery to Python: Extracting a Reusable <code>sources-manifest.json</code></h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            How do you go from looking at a web page full of map links (like <code>vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm#FinalFantasy</code> or a StrategyWiki Table of Contents) to downloading them with Python—<strong>without</strong> manually typing 50 filenames into a one-off script? We use a two-step reusable pattern: first extract a declarative <code>sources-manifest.json</code> (either from the browser console or via Python HTML link discovery), and then feed that manifest to a generic Python downloader.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>Method A — Browser DevTools Console One-Liner (Any Atlas or Wiki Page):</strong> Open Chrome DevTools (<code>F12</code> → <strong>Console</strong>) on any VGMaps or StrategyWiki page, set <code>GAME_PREFIX</code> to the game you want (e.g., <code>\"FinalFantasy-\"</code>, <code>\"DragonWarrior-\"</code>, or <code>\"ChronoTrigger-\"</code>), and run:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>// Run in Browser DevTools Console (F12) on https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm\nconst GAME_PREFIX = \"FinalFantasy-\"; // Change to any game prefix on the page\nconst maps = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"a[href]\")]\n  .map(a => ({ url: a.href, name: a.getAttribute(\"href\").split(\"/\").pop(), label: a.textContent.trim() }))\n  .filter(x => x.name.startsWith(GAME_PREFIX) && /\\.(png|gif)$/i.test(x.name));\n\nconsole.table(maps);\ncopy(JSON.stringify({ gameId: \"ff1\", maps }, null, 2)); // Copies JSON manifest to clipboard!</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>Method B — Automatic Python HTML Link Discovery (Zero Manual Copy-Paste):</strong> Even better, our reusable Python harvester includes an <code>HTMLParser</code> link-discovery mode (<code>--discover-vgmaps</code> and <code>--discover-wiki</code>). You give it the index URL and a filename prefix or wiki slug, and Python automatically parses the HTML DOM, resolves relative URLs with <code>urllib.parse.urljoin</code>, categorizes every asset into <code>maps/</code>, <code>ui_shops/</code>, <code>manuals/</code>, and <code>audio/</code>, and saves <code>sources-manifest.json</code>!\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🐍 3. The Reusable, Game-Agnostic Python Harvester (<code>harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code>)</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Retro map and music archives (such as VGMaps and Zophar) reject Python's default <code>Python-urllib/3.x</code> header with <code>HTTP 403 Forbidden</code>. Below is the complete, <strong>reusable</strong> <code>harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code> utility. It works for <strong>any</strong> classic JRPG via CLI flags (<code>--out-dir</code>, <code>--vgmaps-index</code>, <code>--map-prefix</code>, <code>--wiki-slug</code>, or <code>--manifest</code>), spoofs a desktop Chrome <code>User-Agent</code>, enforces polite rate limiting, inspects PNG dimensions right from the 24-byte IHDR header, and writes a verifiable <code>download-receipt.json</code>:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nharvest_jrpg_assets.py — Reusable Multi-Source Classic JRPG Asset Harvester.\nWorks for any game by passing --vgmaps-index + --map-prefix + --wiki-slug, or a --manifest JSON file.\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse, hashlib, json, os, re, struct, time, urllib.parse, urllib.request\nfrom html.parser import HTMLParser\n\nHEADERS = {\n    \"User-Agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/128.0.0.0 Safari/537.36\",\n    \"Accept\": \"text/html,application/xhtml+xml,image/avif,image/webp,*/*;q=0.8\",\n}\n\nclass LinkExtractor(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self, base_url: str):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.base_url = base_url\n        self.links = []\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        if tag.lower() == \"a\":\n            href = dict(attrs).get(\"href\")\n            if href:\n                self.links.append(urllib.parse.urljoin(self.base_url, href.split(\"#\")[0]))\n\ndef fetch_bytes(url: str, timeout: int = 30) -> bytes:\n    req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers=HEADERS)\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:\n        return resp.read()\n\ndef png_dimensions(data: bytes):\n    if len(data) >= 24 and data[:8] == b\"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\":\n        w, h = struct.unpack(\">II\", data[16:24])\n        return {\"width\": w, \"height\": h}\n    return None\n\ndef discover_vgmaps(index_url: str, prefix: str) -> list[dict]:\n    html = fetch_bytes(index_url).decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\")\n    parser = LinkExtractor(index_url)\n    parser.feed(html)\n    seen, items = set(), []\n    for url in parser.links:\n        fname = os.path.basename(urllib.parse.urlparse(url).path)\n        if fname.startswith(prefix) and fname.lower().endswith((\".png\", \".gif\")) and url not in seen:\n            seen.add(url)\n            items.append({\"category\": \"maps\", \"url\": url, \"filename\": fname})\n    return items\n\ndef discover_strategywiki(wiki_slug: str) -> list[dict]:\n    base = f\"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/{wiki_slug}\"\n    subpages = [\"\", \"Table_of_Contents\", \"Walkthrough\", \"Shops\", \"Weapons\", \"Armor\", \"Spells\", \"Enemies\", \"Characters\"]\n    return [{\n        \"category\": \"ui_shops\",\n        \"url\": f\"{base}/{sub}\" if sub else base,\n        \"filename\": f\"{wiki_slug.replace('/', '_')}_{sub or 'Overview'}.html\"\n    } for sub in subpages]\n\ndef harvest(out_dir: str, entries: list[dict], delay: float = 0.25) -> list[dict]:\n    receipt = []\n    for item in entries:\n        subdir = os.path.join(out_dir, item.get(\"category\", \"misc\"))\n        os.makedirs(subdir, exist_ok=True)\n        dest = os.path.join(subdir, item[\"filename\"])\n        data = fetch_bytes(item[\"url\"])\n        with open(dest, \"wb\") as f:\n            f.write(data)\n        sha = hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()\n        dims = png_dimensions(data)\n        rec = {**item, \"localPath\": dest, \"bytes\": len(data), \"sha256\": sha, \"dimensions\": dims}\n        receipt.append(rec)\n        dim_str = f\" ({dims['width']}x{dims['height']})\" if dims else \"\"\n        print(f\"[OK] {item['category']}/{item['filename']}{dim_str} -> sha256:{sha[:12]}\")\n        time.sleep(delay)\n    with open(os.path.join(out_dir, \"download-receipt.json\"), \"w\") as f:\n        json.dump(receipt, f, indent=2)\n    return receipt\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Reusable Classic JRPG Asset Harvester\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--out-dir\", required=True, help=\"Target private workspace (e.g. ~/Downloads/ff1)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--vgmaps-index\", help=\"VGMaps atlas index URL (e.g. https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--map-prefix\", help=\"Filename prefix on VGMaps (e.g. FinalFantasy- or DragonWarrior-)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--wiki-slug\", help=\"StrategyWiki game slug (e.g. Final_Fantasy or Dragon_Warrior)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--extra-url\", action=\"append\", default=[], help=\"Extra CAT:FILENAME:URL triples (manuals/audio)\")\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    out_dir = os.path.expanduser(args.out_dir)\n    manifest = []\n    if args.vgmaps_index and args.map_prefix:\n        manifest.extend(discover_vgmaps(args.vgmaps_index, args.map_prefix))\n    if args.wiki_slug:\n        manifest.extend(discover_strategywiki(args.wiki_slug))\n    for spec in args.extra_url:\n        cat, fname, url = spec.split(\":\", 2)\n        manifest.append({\"category\": cat, \"filename\": fname, \"url\": url})\n\n    os.makedirs(out_dir, exist_ok=True)\n    with open(os.path.join(out_dir, \"sources-manifest.json\"), \"w\") as f:\n        json.dump(manifest, f, indent=2)\n    harvest(out_dir, manifest)</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Notice how reusable this is: to switch from NES <em>Final Fantasy</em> to <em>Dragon Warrior</em> or SNES <em>Chrono Trigger</em>, you simply change <code>--map-prefix</code> and <code>--wiki-slug</code> without rewriting a single line of Python:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># Example 1: Harvest NES Final Fantasy into ~/Downloads/ff1\npython3 harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/Downloads/ff1 \\\n  --vgmaps-index \"https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm\" \\\n  --map-prefix \"FinalFantasy-\" \\\n  --wiki-slug \"Final_Fantasy\" \\\n  --extra-url \"manuals:CLV-P-NAAAE.pdf:https://www.nintendo.co.jp/clv/manuals/en/pdf/CLV-P-NAAAE.pdf\"\n\n# Example 2: Reuse the exact same script for NES Dragon Warrior\npython3 harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/Downloads/dw1 \\\n  --vgmaps-index \"https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm\" \\\n  --map-prefix \"DragonWarrior-\" \\\n  --wiki-slug \"Dragon_Warrior\"</code></pre>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🤖 4. How to Use RobOS Agents to Run the Harvester &amp; Fire Off the Parser</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            You do not even need to run these scripts manually. In RobOS, you orchestrate the entire acquisition and KGraph ingestion workflow through <strong>RobOS Agents</strong> (via <strong>RobOS Agent Chat</strong>, <strong>Agents Manager</strong>, or your signed-in coding-agent CLI: Google Antigravity <code>agy</code> / Gemini CLI, Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, or GitHub Copilot).\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>Step A — Prompting the Agent to Harvest Assets &amp; Launch <code>jrpg-parse-assets</code>:</strong> Open <strong>RobOS Agent Chat</strong> (or your agent CLI in <code>/home/ndipiazza/source/robos</code>) and send a single structured prompt:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Prompt to RobOS Agent:\n\"1. Use Python to harvest all VGMaps PNGs (prefix FinalFantasy- from https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm),\n   StrategyWiki pages (Final_Fantasy), and the NES Classic Mini manual PDF into /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1.\n 2. Run the /jrpg-parse-assets skill on /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1 to build the Knowledge Graph in\n   /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1, and report the coverage table and what is still missing for a full game.\"</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>Step B — What the RobOS Agent Executes Under the Hood:</strong> When the agent receives this prompt, it activates the repository skill <code>.agents/skills/jrpg-parse-assets/SKILL.md</code> and executes a 4-stage pipeline:\n          </p>\n          <div class=\"mermaid\">\nflowchart LR\n    Prompt[\"1. User Prompt in RobOS Agent Chat<br/>(Target: ~/Downloads/ff1)\"] --> Harvester[\"2. Agent Runs harvest_jrpg_assets.py<br/>(Downloads Maps, Wiki HTML, PDF, Audio)\"]\n    Harvester --> ScanCmd[\"3. jrpg-parser.js scan ~/Downloads/ff1<br/>(Classifies Roles &amp; Skips Remake Pages)\"]\n    ScanCmd --> ParseCmd[\"4. jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1<br/>(Deterministic Tables + robos-lib/ai-agent)\"]\n    ParseCmd --> CovCmd[\"5. jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/kgraphs/ff1<br/>(Emits Completeness Matrix &amp; Blockers)\"]\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"doc-figure\">\n            <img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/jrpg-acquisition-and-rom-pipeline.png\" alt=\"Stage 1: Browser and Python Multi-Source JRPG Asset Acquisition Pipeline\" class=\"doc-img\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\">\n            <div class=\"doc-caption\">Figure 1.1: Stage 1 technical schematic — multi-source browser &amp; Python acquisition pipeline feeding the private ~/Downloads/&lt;game-id&gt; workspace and firing off robos-jrpg-parser.</div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      ",
          "labSteps": [
            "Inspect the target game index page in your browser (or run the DevTools Console snippet) to verify the map filename prefix (e.g. `FinalFantasy-`) and StrategyWiki slug (`Final_Fantasy`)",
            "Run the reusable `harvest_jrpg_assets.py` script with `--out-dir ~/Downloads/ff1 --vgmaps-index https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm --map-prefix FinalFantasy- --wiki-slug Final_Fantasy`",
            "Inspect `~/Downloads/ff1/sources-manifest.json` and `~/Downloads/ff1/download-receipt.json` to confirm SHA-256 hashes and PNG dimensions (`1024x1024` for 64x64 tile maps, `4096x4096` for Overworld)",
            "In RobOS Agent Chat (or your agent CLI), invoke the `jrpg-parse-assets` skill on `~/Downloads/ff1` to fire off `packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js`"
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "How do we make the Python asset downloader reusable across any classic JRPG (such as Final Fantasy, Dragon Warrior, or Chrono Trigger) instead of hardcoding filenames?",
              "options": [
                "By passing the index URL, filename prefix (--map-prefix), and wiki slug (--wiki-slug) to an HTMLParser link-discovery function that generates sources-manifest.json dynamically",
                "By manually renaming every PNG file in MS Paint before running Python",
                "By committing all commercial ROMs directly into the public Git repository",
                "By hardcoding a 500-element array of URLs inside the Godot engine binary"
              ],
              "answer": "By passing the index URL, filename prefix (--map-prefix), and wiki slug (--wiki-slug) to an HTMLParser link-discovery function that generates sources-manifest.json dynamically",
              "explanation": "Using HTMLParser link discovery (or a Browser DevTools manifest extractor) decouples the Python downloader from any single game so the same script harvests any JRPG."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does a default urllib.request.urlopen(url) call fail with HTTP 403 Forbidden on retro archives like VGMaps and Zophar, and how does harvest_jrpg_assets.py fix it?",
              "options": [
                "They block the default Python-urllib User-Agent; passing a standard browser User-Agent header in urllib.request.Request resolves it",
                "They require OAuth2 bearer tokens generated via Google Cloud",
                "They only serve files over FTP rather than HTTPS",
                "They require WebSocket handshakes before downloading PNGs"
              ],
              "answer": "They block the default Python-urllib User-Agent; passing a standard browser User-Agent header in urllib.request.Request resolves it",
              "explanation": "Passing a standard desktop browser User-Agent header with polite rate limiting allows deterministic Python downloads without 403 blocks."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-02-jrpg-kgraph-parser-and-gap-matrix",
          "title": "Module 2: Firing Off robos-jrpg-parser via RobOS Agents (scan, parse, coverage, art) & Gap Analysis",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Learn how RobOS Agents execute packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js (scan, parse, coverage, plan, art, publish), how the parser combines deterministic table extraction with robos-lib/ai-agent quote-verified LLM extraction, and how to audit the 11-Area Completeness Gap Matrix.",
          "lessonHtml": "\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🧠 1. How RobOS Agents Fire Off <code>jrpg-parser.js</code> (Step-by-Step CLI &amp; Agent Protocol)</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Once <code>harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code> populates <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1</code>, how does the RobOS Agent turn those raw PNGs, HTML pages, and PDFs into a validated Knowledge Graph? The agent follows the <strong><code>jrpg-parse-assets</code> skill</strong> (<code>.agents/skills/jrpg-parse-assets/SKILL.md</code>), which drives <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js</code> through four explicit commands:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># STEP 1: Scan the downloaded folder to inspect every file's inferred role and dimensions\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js scan /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1\n\n# Example scan output:\n# use   world-map      4096x4096  FinalFantasy-World.png\n# use   area-map       1024x1024  FinalFantasy-Coneria.png\n# use   area-map       2048x1024  FinalFantasy-CastleConeria.png\n# use   walkthrough               Final_Fantasy_Walkthrough.html\n# use   shop-screen               Final_Fantasy_Shops.html\n# skip  walkthrough               Final_Fantasy_GBA_DawnOfSouls.html  (Other edition)\n\n# STEP 2: (Optional) Override any misclassified file or exclude remake pages via manifest.json\ncat &lt;&lt; 'EOF' &gt; /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/manifest.json\n{\n  \"files\": {\n    \"Final_Fantasy_GBA_DawnOfSouls.html\": { \"use\": false, \"note\": \"GBA remake prices differ from NES\" }\n  }\n}\nEOF\n\n# STEP 3: Fire off the hybrid parser (Deterministic Code + Active RobOS AI Agent)\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1 \\\n  --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1 \\\n  --title \"Final Fantasy\" \\\n  --edition \"NES\" \\\n  --manifest /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/manifest.json\n\n# STEP 4: Print the coverage report and \"Still to collect by hand\" gap list\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js coverage --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1</code></pre>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🤖 2. Inside <code>jrpg-parser.js parse</code>: Deterministic Code + Quote-Verified Agent Extraction</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Why does <code>jrpg-parser.js parse</code> accept <code>--provider &lt;id&gt;</code> (defaulting to your active CLI in <code>~/.config/robos/ai-provider.json</code>: <code>antigravity</code> / <code>agy</code>, <code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, or <code>copilot</code>) and <code>--no-agent</code>? Because <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser</code> is a <strong>two-tier hybrid parser</strong>:\n          </p>\n          <div class=\"mermaid\">\nflowchart TD\n    InputDir[\"~/Downloads/ff1<br/>(Maps, HTML Tables, PDF, Audio, ROM Markdown)\"] --> Scan[\"1. pipeline.scan() &amp; classify.js<br/>(SHA-256 + Image Dimensions + Edition Filter)\"]\n    Scan --> Tier1[\"2. Tier 1: Deterministic Parsers (charts.js, maps.js)<br/>Reads HTML/Markdown Tables for Weapons, Armor, Spells,<br/>Shops, Monsters, Encounters, Class Stats &amp; Chests\"]\n    Scan --> Tier2[\"3. Tier 2: RobOS Agent Extraction (robos-lib/ai-agent)<br/>Extracts Story Milestones, Battle Rules, Places &amp; Layouts<br/>from Unstructured Guide Prose (Cached in .cache/agent)\"]\n    Tier2 --> QuoteGate[\"4. Anti-Hallucination Quote Gate<br/>Every agent record MUST include a verbatim quote.<br/>If quote is not in source text -> Record is DROPPED!\"]\n    Tier1 --> KGraphOut[\"5. kgraph.js + SHACL Validator<br/>~/kgraphs/ff1/knowledge-graph.jsonld + coverage.json\"]\n    QuoteGate --> KGraphOut\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            This architecture gives you the best of both worlds:\n          </p>\n          <ul style=\"margin:8px 0 12px 20px; font-size:13px; line-height:1.7;\">\n            <li><strong>Zero LLM math errors on tables:</strong> All numeric tables (80 weapons/armor, 64 spells, 124 enemies, 50 shops) are parsed by deterministic code in <code>lib/charts.js</code>, which also cross-checks numbers across files and reports any disagreements.</li>\n            <li><strong>Zero hallucinated lore or rules:</strong> When <code>jrpg-parser.js</code> calls <code>robos-lib/ai-agent</code> to extract story milestones or battle rules from prose, it requires a verbatim <code>quote</code> field on every JSON object. If the agent invents a fact whose quote does not exist verbatim in the local file, <code>jrpg-parser.js</code> discards it automatically.</li>\n            <li><strong>Free, repeatable reruns:</strong> Agent replies are cached by prompt SHA-256 in <code>~/kgraphs/ff1/.cache/agent/</code>, so re-running <code>jrpg-parser.js parse</code> after adding new files only queries the agent for new content.</li>\n          </ul>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">🎨 3. Firing Off Clean-Room Art Generation (<code>jrpg-generate-art</code> Skill) &amp; Clean Publishing</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Once the KGraph nodes exist, the agent can also invoke the <strong><code>jrpg-generate-art</code> skill</strong> (<code>.agents/skills/jrpg-generate-art/SKILL.md</code>), which uses <code>packages/robos-lib/ai-image.js</code> (through your signed-in Antigravity <code>agy</code> CLI and Gemini image model) plus <code>tools/nesify.py</code> to generate clean-room sprites, battle backdrops, and maps:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># 1. Preview all image generation jobs planned from KGraph nodes\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js plan --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1\n\n# 2. Generate a small batch first (3 images) to inspect quality\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js art --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1 --limit 3\n\n# 3. Export a clean-room shareable KGraph (strips all source filenames, hashes, and verbatim quotes)\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js publish \\\n  --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1 \\\n  --to /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/ff1-clean</code></pre>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">📊 4. The 11-Area Completeness Gap Matrix (Why Wiki Scrapes Alone Cannot Run a Game)</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            After the first parse of the VGMaps PNGs, StrategyWiki pages, and Nintendo manual PDF, the RobOS agent audited <code>coverage.json</code> and compared the Knowledge Graph against what a real game engine needs to run battles, level-ups, magic, walking, and story triggers. This audit revealed five critical blockers:\n          </p>\n          <table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:12px; margin:12px 0;\">\n            <thead>\n              <tr style=\"background:#21262d; color:#f0f6fc;\">\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Area</th>\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Have from Initial Scrape</th>\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Missing for Playable Game</th>\n                <th style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Effect</th>\n              </tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Weapons, armor, shops</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Stats and prices for 80 pieces, 50 shops</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">5 disputed numbers across ports</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#3fb950;\"><strong>Ready</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Battle rules</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">37 formulas, including NES bugs</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Unconfirmed author &amp; 3 NES quirks</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f1e05a;\"><strong>Usable once verified</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Enemies</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">124 with HP, XP, gold, weakness</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">ATK, DEF, ACC, Hits, CRT, EVA, M.D, MOR, spells</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f85149;\"><strong>Blocks battles</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Encounters</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Nothing</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Which enemy groups appear where (8-slot zone table)</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f85149;\"><strong>Blocks battles</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Classes</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">12 with upgrades</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Starting stats, level-up growth, XP table</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f85149;\"><strong>Blocks progression</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Spells</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">64 with level, price, school, effect</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Power and accuracy, which the formulas need</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f85149;\"><strong>Blocks magic</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>Maps</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">27 real map files, 28 generated images</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">16x16 tile grids, walkable tiles, doors, stairs, warps</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f85149;\"><strong>Blocks walking</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n              <tr>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\"><strong>World contents</strong></td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Partial chest locations from StrategyWiki</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d;\">Exact chest (x,y) + contents, NPC positions, dialogue</td>\n                <td style=\"padding:8px 10px; border:1px solid #30363d; color:#f85149;\"><strong>Blocks the story</strong></td>\n              </tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"doc-figure\">\n            <img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/jrpg-kgraph-parser-and-gap-analysis.png\" alt=\"Stage 2: robos-jrpg-parser Ingestion, KGraph Ontology and Completeness Gap Matrix\" class=\"doc-img\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\">\n            <div class=\"doc-caption\">Figure 2.1: Stage 2 architecture — OSLC JSON-LD node types produced by robos-jrpg-parser and the Completeness Audit Table.</div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      ",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run `node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js scan ~/Downloads/ff1` to verify how the classifier assigns roles (`world-map`, `area-map`, `shop-screen`, `walkthrough`, `document`, `music`) and flags non-NES editions",
            "Run `node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/Downloads/ff1 --out ~/kgraphs/ff1 --title \"Final Fantasy\" --edition \"NES\"` to run deterministic table extraction + quote-verified RobOS Agent extraction",
            "Run `node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/kgraphs/ff1` to inspect `coverage.json` and identify the 5 gameplay blockers",
            "Run `node --test packages/robos-test/tests/jrpg-parser/unit.test.js` and verify all 63 unit tests pass using synthetic clean-room fixtures"
          ],
